[Music]  so one week from today  as the nation pauses for memorial day we  remember  the men and women who've given their  lives in defense of our country  well cbs's steve hartman is going to be  on the road again on the holiday for a  special reason it's going to be his  second  taps across america in fact steve's with  us live right now from his home in  upstate new york uh obviously it sounds  pretty self-explanatory but just in case  what  is taps across america  taps across america is where we're  encouraging buglers and trumpet players  across the country  to go out on their front porches and  play taps at 3 p.m  local time and we're asking people who  hear them  to play taps to go out on their front  porches  stand at attention hand over heart and  remember  where did the idea for this come from  well several years ago i did a story  about a guy named don britton who lived  in tacoma washington  and he would stand on his balcony at  sunset every day and he would play taps  and it was very moving but what struck  me most what brought me to tears  in the moment was as he played everybody  living around him  was coming out on their porches standing  in their front yards  and listening in honor and  that feeling of community that  that everybody coming together for a  single purpose really got to me for some  reason and ever since then i thought  wouldn't be great if we did this across  the country  and then when the pandemic struck last  year and all  memorial day celebrations were mostly  cancelled  i thought this is the time so how did it  go last year with those unusual  circumstances to have people all over  the country at the exact same time  doing this very special moment  it blew us away i think people wanted  this you know in our busy hectic lives  just to take a moment  and um to pause and remember  and to remember what the holiday is all  about i think so often  the meaning has become hamburgers and  hot dogs and it's not really why we have  the holiday in the first place and this  was a way for everybody  to uh be brought back to the purpose of  the holidays so for that reason  people sent us videos galore and we want  more we want we no matter where you're  standing in america at 3 p.m  you know with the highest mount in the  lowest valley we want people to hear  taps and  we want the people playing to send us  their videos and you can sign up if you  do play  at taps and you can  send us your videos and we're going to  put a lot of them on tv so is this  anybody who's ever played a trumpet or a  bugle how does that work  yeah we had a one guy who's uh he's  never  played an instrument in his life he's in  the 60s and he he got a trumpet so he  could play this year so yeah  we need a good sincere effort we really  any instrument would do  you know anything up from a kazoo we  don't want that  but um anything you can play in  reverence you know would be acceptable  yeah well steve so give us that website  one more time if folks want to learn  more about how to participate and  see how it all works sure it's  cbsnews.com  that's the easy part slash that's the  hard part because you never know if it's  slasher backslash  taps cbsnews.com taps to sign up or  learn more about it  steve right on and don't forget steve is  with us every friday night on cbs news  going on the road telling  stories about incredible people doing  incredible things all over the country  steve thanks so much we can't wait to  see how it turns out next week  thank you so much all right alex let's  send it on over to you

